ual point of view i feel  like the club's only compliment and i  don't feel  like my involvement in euro club neither  as a member last year nor as a  leadership person this year  um does take away too much time from  from the studies  okay i might add to that  i i agree totally with everything mark  has said i think it actually complements  your studies  and i think that leadership is often a  balance between  multitasking with empathy and  ruthless prioritization  and since we're here in the mba to  advance our course as leaders and learn  how to be leaders this is literally just  a practice test and a practice test  wherein code failure doesn't come with  very huge consequences so this is a safe  space for you to try out these skills  and become a leader  and so to that um and  you find people in five clubs and they  are at the top of the class and there  are people in  two clubs and they are very okay with  both  the academic and their social experience  so  again to mark's point it's it's it's a  self-directed agenda  um i can add that our leadership teams  are usually pretty big  and so people can help take off  weight on you if things are particularly  heavy  for a certain week and vice versa and so  i think there's a lot of balance that  way by sharing the burden  um and i do think being part of  leadership  um and being part of these clubs can  complement your studies i had  um i had to write a paper on this  company named silco watch space in kenya  and i knew that someone knew this  founder because i was part of the abc  club and were able to talk to them and  get like i think a great paper  because of it so i think they're like  very tactical perks but  uh because of what we talked about  earlier about people at the gsb just  being so kind and  welcoming and helpful that um your  members of your leadership team can help  you  when when the load is particularly heavy  yeah i think i have nothing to add you  all said it perfectly  thank you all we have another great  question from mikhail  who writes were there any cultural  differences that you struggled with in  stanford specifically  or in california in general like how to  start a conversation  how to stay online things like that or  anything else  and if there were any cultural  differences that you struggled with how  did you  manage or overcome them  i can go  um i'm a little bit of a philosopher  and so and so i  i generally believe that the pathway  to whatever destination it is that we  um get to is necessarily going to have  turns obstacles potholes and corn that  each and every one of those  things adds extra to the experience and  so  i have savored those differences  actually and learned from them and i  find out that  sometimes what you learn from those  differences are  um the actually transferable skills when  it comes to things like managing upwards  in the workplace or managing a very  diverse workforce or things like that  and um so personally i'd say i've  learned  mostly by observation seeing how other  people from other contexts to it  but sometimes it's actually just been  asking a blunt question and saying  hey i realized that we do things  differently where i come from  i knew people do things in this way  um can you please explain the rationale  um  and then you create some blended  um solution for yourself because that's  the beauty of all of this  i think because california is so  welcoming and specifically the gsb  you're not expected to do it  in a particular we expected to do it  your way  as long as um there's regard for  community  and other people's feelings and how  um the you know your your actions land  on others so um  i i'd say that it's still a work in  progress  and maybe the two years of an mba is  actually too short for you to be able to  fully overcome all of all of these  things but actually think it's  part of the mba experience that you will  enjoy  as an international student because many  people would also ask you hey  i see you guys do things in a different  way  i'm actually enamored with it can you  tell me about it so  it's a little bit of give and take  anybody else want to chime in on this  question  yeah i think i totally agree with josh  there's a couple of like ground norms  that are established pretty quickly at  the gsb and then within that it's really  about  an individual and how you show up as an  individual and that that matters most  and  this is an environment where you get to  know each other as individuals rather  than as you know  whatever stereotypes you have so i think  it's actually an incredible opportunity  to bridge those kind of cultural gaps  that sometimes may exist  yeah i think one aspect that i will add  is  i feel like the gsb is a lot about  self-disclosure  i think it's about making yourself known  and having other people know you  um and i think that's something that i  might be  too generalizing here but i feel like in  in the german culture where i grew up  that is not as much of a focus point in  interpersonal dynamics as it is  um maybe in the us and california at the  gsb in particular i don't know where  when the funnel is really um active but  i feel like the level of self-disclosure  and like how  vulnerable you show yourself to each  other  um is really something that was somewhat  new to me at the same time  was the kind of foundation for a lot of  very meaningful  relationships and friendships  awesome thank you all we have another  great question  from sabrina who writes what do you feel  that the impact of the african europe  clubs has been  on gsb's campus um sabrina  adds that she would also love to hear  what you all are most excited about for  the africa  and europe clubs in regard to their  futures  for the africa business club i think i  mentioned during the introduction that  we organized  um as an independent event because of  some logistical  um concerns the  stanford africa business forum which is  the largest africa run  um student event  sorry largest student-run event on  campus  and during that time the gsb  literally gives us the entire campus  to make use of you know the facilities  the faculty are very helpful  um the staff on the back end  everything um and so this has  shall we say created a a bit of  uh deeper knowledge a deeper interest in  african issues  amongst both staff and um  people who otherwise might not might  just meet an african  every other month or every year you know  um stuff like that so i'd say that in  that respect the adc has had a huge  influence here secondly  i i think that there's a greater  engagement  with african issues from the diversity  perspective  um so for example with the nsa's  protests in nigeria the other day  um everybody who  had a nigerian address attached to them  or maybe the identity got an email from  why does stanford school not even just  just be saying  hey we understand this must be a  stressful time you know  things like that but what i heard from  the back end or from the back side  is that um a lot of it had to do  with you know conversations with the gsb  um uh leadership or  gsb faculty so it means that  our effect has not just been on the jsv  it has also extended as far as  um stanford um  i would also say that because  we have created a conduit of african  food here on campus  and now everybody can call the lady who  supplies  um the food there's a renaissance of  african food around campus so before  long you might go to a canteen here  and the only kind of rice you'll be able  to buy is jello fries  so  and i can quickly chime in on the second  part of what i'm really excited about  for the african business club um i think  eugene and josh have done such a great  job  um pushing forward cross-campus  engagement uh not only at stanford  between different schools like the law  school and med school people that are  interested in  africa but also other schools like  berkeley and are just next door or given  the virtual world  uh just other schools around the us and  i think that's a really  beautiful opportunity to build community  that's this burst and so i'm really  excited about that  yeah i think in terms of in terms of  impact it is around the goals that we  stated you know it's um  it's social it's getting people together  on the social level get  get get people connected it's making  sure  people who are interested in working in  europe have the resources have to have  the capabilities feel ready  to to jump into the job search there and  um  and yeah and then i think thirdly  probably around admissions making sure  that applicants are there and that  people who are accepted here or  feel welcome and you know who know who  to reach out to  and um and get connected with it's kind  of the impact we we try and have  and there's still so much so much we  want to do on all three levels so that's  what i'm excited about i'm excited to  get more europeans here i'm excited to  get more people to work in europe  and i'm excited to create a stronger  sense of community of everybody who's in  any way  interested or connected to europe and  make sure you know people know each  other and  not only know each other on a  superficial level for that kind of as i  was saying before for the rolodex but  actually get to know each other  as as a person  yeah i'll close out with saying in terms  of impact  um there has been this program over the  summer  that um basically matched up people from  the europe club leadership  with admits who had questions about the  gsb  over the summer and um we we all had  this jetson leadership team  and just like connecting with the folks  that i spoke to back then  it seemed like that these touch points  have been incredibly helpful for them so  i think we just want to  keep keep being available as a resource  to provide insights both for people who  want to come here but also for people  who are here  um what i'm most excited about um  i feel like probably  one aspect is a professional aspect  there have been a few people from  our class who are domestic american  students who  got really excited about the prospect of  living in europe  and i feel like seeing them and hearing  the feedback that basically our sessions  provided helpful insights to them  it was really rewarding and i feel like  we want to continue to do that  awesome thank you all so much so i see  we're coming up to the top of the hour  we have one final question  i want to thank our attendees for all of  the really really thoughtful questions  they've submitted  if you have any outstanding questions  please feel free to reach out to our  office  or attend more of the diversity and  leadership events going on this week  to learn more about student experiences  at the gsb  um you'll also see a poll pop up so if  you could  please fill out the poll so we can  continue improving  oh my apologies there is no poll on this  event  but if you have any additional questions  please feel free to reach out to us  our final question for this session is  looking back if you could go back in  time what advice would you give yourself  as you were getting ready for business  school  i'll go fast again um  i would say choose tomfood  okay seriously um  [Music]  i think that um  the advice i'd give myself is to come  more open-minded um  i came here with the thesis about what  exactly i wanted  and it changed  maybe like after four or five weeks i  can see a scenario where if i had been  more open-minded earlier  um i might even have absorbed a lot more  than i did  um before i figured out that i was doing  it the wrong way and yes  um already made the point earlier that  you you need to be focused  about the range of things that you want  to get out of the business school  experience because this is like  candyland  and you can get pushed in a million  different directions  but um still having that  receptiveness to the many aha moments  that can occur in many different  settings  to set you down the path that maybe you  were made for  and i i i think that's what i tell  myself  for me uh if i could go back to myself  pre gsb i would take more time off  um i think i've never been as busy as i  have been at the gsd  and it's it's a wonderful thing because  you're seeing people you're  going to meetings you're meeting like  great speakers and all that stuff  but it is exhausting too and  so take some time take care of yourself  before you jump into this  whirlwind experience  i think similarly to ori um get ready  just to  stretch yourself so whatever you need to  to get ready for the for this experience  i think i think take the time and get  into the right  headspace that once you're here uh  you're you're ready to learn to engage  in  and bring your best self  yeah i'll like everything that has been  said i think in addition  i think it helps to come in with a  hypothesis of what you want to do  during the two years and after the gsb  at the same time i  emphasize or it resonates a lot when i  never said that um you should let give  yourselves  the kind of room for serendipity um i  think the gsp can be a rocket ship that  takes you  on the career you never even thought was  possible it can be a playground where  you try a few things and  something is worth some things don't but  ultimately it seems like  most if not all of the alums have found  this experience incredibly  rewarding no matter what their journey  has been so um  don't like come in with some goals but  don't get too focused on them and let  basically let yourself be inspired by  the experience and by the classmates  that you come across  awesome thank you all so much for  sharing your experiences with us today  and thank you so much to our  attendees for joining us as we wrap up  as i said before feel free to reach out  to us if you have more questions  attend more of our diversity and  leadership events or continue your  journey at mystanford.mba  thank you so much for joining us today  and i hope everyone has a great rest of  your day

